<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">8 January 1918 · Washington, D.C.</p><h1 class="sp-title">Wilson's Fourteen Points</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>Nine months after the United States entered the First World War, and weeks after the Bolsheviks had published the Allies' secret treaties, President Woodrow Wilson set out to Congress the terms on which he believed a lasting peace could be built.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>Open covenants of peace, openly arrived at, after which there shall be no private international understandings of any kind...</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Address to a joint session of Congress, 8 January 1918.</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Fourteen_Points_Speech" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">Wikisource</span></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>The Fourteen Points, which called for freedom of the seas, free trade, arms reduction, an independent Poland, autonomy for the peoples of Austria-Hungary and the Ottoman Empire, and a general association of nations, became the basis on which Germany sought an armistice in 1918. At the Paris Peace Conference many of them were compromised, and although the League of Nations was founded, the United States Senate refused to ratify the Treaty of Versailles or to join it.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">Armistice of 11 November 1918</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a></div></div></section>
